# Copy to .env and fill locally. NEVER commit .env. All personal-data services must be EU-region.
#
# EVERY NAME IN THIS FILE IS READ BY CODE, AND EVERY NAME CODE READS IS IN THIS FILE.
# `tools/spec-audit/env-check.mjs` enforces both directions and runs in `npm run all`. This is not
# tidiness: the previous version advertised seventeen variables nothing had ever read (MAILER_*,
# POSTAL_*, IDENTITY_*, QES_PROVIDER, STRIPE_SECRET_KEY …) while omitting the five SCRAPER_* seams
# the worker actually requires in production, and named WORKFLOW_ENGINE where the code reads
# SCRAPER_WORKFLOW_ENGINE. An operator configuring from it would have set a careful, complete-looking
# environment that the process ignored.
#
# A variable belongs here when a deployment may need to set it — including one whose only reader is
# today `scripts/readiness.mjs`. Adapters that do not exist yet (a real object store, a real mailer)
# get their variables in the commit that builds them, not before: a name in this file is a promise
# that setting it does something.

# --- Core ---
NODE_ENV=development
# Testing alpha ONLY (ADR-028): serves ONE fixture identity + simulated transitions. It activates
# ONLY when NODE_ENV is development or test — an unset or unrecognised NODE_ENV throws rather than
# assuming development (allow-list, not deny-list). Never set in any deployed environment.
SCRAPER_DEV_FIXTURES=1
PORT=3900
# PORT note: :3000 belongs to the main Next.js Scraper web app on dev machines.
# Where apps/web-next reaches the API server-side (next.config.mjs, src/lib/api.ts, the Akte upload
# route). Defaults to http://localhost:3900 when unset.
API_URL=http://localhost:3900

# --- Persistence and durable time ---
# Both are REQUIRED to be exactly these values outside development/test: assertApiStartupSafe()
# refuses to boot otherwise. `memory` is a process-lifetime Map — every request, evidence record and
# provenance entry is lost on restart, and auth and the ops queue do not even mount. An empty
# scheduler means no durable Frist timer is ever armed: requests send, and nothing ever expires a
# provisional deadline or gives up waiting for a delivery receipt. Both are legitimate for a
# standalone local alpha and for nothing else.
SCRAPER_REPOSITORY=prisma          # prisma (required outside dev) | memory (standalone alpha only)
SCRAPER_SCHEDULER=pgboss           # pgboss (required outside dev) | empty (no durable timers)
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://scraper:scraper@localhost:5432/scraper3
# The Frist-timer backend. NOTE THE PREFIX — the worker reads SCRAPER_WORKFLOW_ENGINE, and the
# unprefixed WORKFLOW_ENGINE this file used to name was read by nothing.
SCRAPER_WORKFLOW_ENGINE=pgboss     # pgboss (default) | bullmq (needs bullmq+ioredis) | temporal (target, placeholder)
REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6379   # bullmq engine only

# --- Crypto at rest (envelope encryption, CLAUDE.md §4) ---
# REQUIRED outside development/test — the API refuses to boot otherwise (assertApiStartupSafe).
# Empty selects the dev KEK resolver, which only runs under NODE_ENV development|test.
SCRAPER_KEK_MODE=                  # env | empty (dev resolver, allow-listed)
# EnvKekResolver reads SCRAPER_KEK_<KEKREF>; "user" is the only kekRef in use today.
SCRAPER_KEK_USER=                  # base64 of exactly 32 bytes — the KEK for kekRef "user"

# --- CORS (REQUIRED outside development/test; comma-separated origins) ---
SCRAPER_CORS_ORIGINS=              # e.g. https://app.scraper.example

# --- Residency (CLAUDE.md §3) ---
# The one legacy name that is genuinely live: both the worker's startup guard and readiness read it,
# and the worker refuses to start on anything but "eu" in EVERY environment. Not a preference.
MODEL_REGION=eu

# --- Provider seams (apps/worker/src/config.ts REQUIRED_REAL_PROVIDERS) ---
# The worker refuses to start in production unless all six name a real adapter — unset counts as
# stub, because an unset selector falls back to one. Five of the six are not dereferenced yet: the
# factory is stage 4, and until it lands the worker refuses a non-dev boot outright rather than
# accepting these values and ignoring them. SCRAPER_OBJECT_STORE is the exception — it IS read, by
# `createObjectStore()`, and neither of its values is a stub.
SCRAPER_MAILER=stub                # stub | (real adapter — not yet built)
SCRAPER_POSTAL=stub                # stub | letterxpress
SCRAPER_TIMESTAMPER=simulated      # simulated | openapi (qualified eIDAS timestamps)
SCRAPER_IDENTITY=stub              # stub | postident
SCRAPER_DOC_SANDBOX=stub           # stub | refusing (services/doc-sandbox)
SCRAPER_OBJECT_STORE=fs            # fs (posture A: the operator's own disk) | s3 (postures B/C)

# --- Worker operational limits ---
RAW_RESPONSE_RETENTION_DAYS=30     # docs/03: raw response documents purge after normalisation
GATEWAY_MAX_SENDS_PER_CONTROLLER_PER_HOUR=10   # a per-controller flood is an anomaly, not throughput
TEMPLATES_DIR=                     # defaults to the repo's templates/ — set when packaged

# --- Corpus CLI (packages/corpus-cli) ---
# Who is recorded in the activation ledger when this node's operator activates or deactivates a
# playbook (migration 0019). On posture A that is the data subject themselves. Falls back to $USER;
# `corpus:activate` refuses rather than recording an empty actor. `SCRAPER_ROOT` overrides where the
# CLI looks for playbooks/ and templates/ — only needed when they are not under a checkout.
SCRAPER_ACTOR=

# --- Evidence object store (apps/worker/src/providers/object-store/README.md) ---
# Holds the rendered copy of every letter (the artefact the evidence chain hashes, CLAUDE.md §6) and
# raw controller documents until their retention window expires (CLAUDE.md §4). `pnpm --filter
# @scraper/worker probe:store` writes, reads back, verifies the SHA-256 and deletes — readiness runs
# that round trip itself for `fs`, and points here for `s3`.
#
# SCRAPER_OBJECT_STORE=fs — the only variable needed. Absolute outside development; a relative root
# resolves against the process working directory, which for a systemd unit is not where you think.
OBJECT_STORE_FS_ROOT=./.data/objects
#
# SCRAPER_OBJECT_STORE=s3 — path-style addressing; the bucket must be private and encrypted at rest.
# The region is refused unless it is EU (CLAUDE.md §3): AWS `eu-*` plus the named non-AWS EU regions
# in s3.ts. That check reads a STRING — where the host physically is stays a human ☐ in readiness.
OBJECT_STORE_ENDPOINT=             # S3-compatible, EU region (e.g. https://s3.fr-par.scw.cloud)
OBJECT_STORE_BUCKET=
OBJECT_STORE_REGION=               # e.g. fr-par | nl-ams | fsn1 | eu-central-1
OBJECT_STORE_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
OBJECT_STORE_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=

# --- Real vendor credentials (contract-gated, ADR §4; apps/worker/src/providers/real-providers.ts) ---
# Each adapter throws CREDENTIALS_MISSING without its keys. No account exists yet, so none of these
# has run against a live endpoint.
LETTERXPRESS_USER=
LETTERXPRESS_APIKEY=
LETTERXPRESS_MODE=sandbox          # sandbox | live — live additionally requires the OQ-11 receipt fetch
LETTERXPRESS_BASE=                 # defaults to the LXP v3 SANDBOX host
QTSP_TOKEN=                        # Openapi/InfoCert eIDAS timestamps
# Unset means the vendor's TEST host, and the adapter anchors SIMULATED against it — no statutory
# clock, by design. An anchor is QUALIFIED only against a host on QUALIFIED_QTSP_HOSTS in
# apps/worker/src/providers/real-providers.ts, because "this endpoint issues qualified timestamps" is
# a fact about a company on an EU Trusted List, not a string an operator may set to anything.
QTSP_BASE=
POSTIDENT_CLIENT_ID=
POSTIDENT_PASSWORD=
POSTIDENT_BASE=                    # defaults to the POSTIDENT test host
